---
# ACME DNS-01 issuer for public hostnames served by Traefik (IN-16 follow-up).
#
# WHY DNS-01 AND NOT HTTP-01: nothing in this lab is publicly reachable on :443. Inbound traffic
# arrives only through a Cloudflare Tunnel, and the hostnames are additionally gated by Cloudflare
# Access. An HTTP-01 challenge would need Let's Encrypt to fetch a token over the public internet,
# which cannot happen. DNS-01 only needs a TXT record in the zone, so it works regardless of
# reachability — and regardless of the hostname being proxied by Cloudflare.
#
# WHY THE TOKEN IS A PARAMETER AND NOT PULLED FROM VAULT: cert-manager is part of init-resources
# and deploys BEFORE Vault exists (see the env config comment: "deploys BEFORE vault so its
# Certificate/Issuer manifests have the CRDs available"). An ESO-delivered secret would therefore
# have an ordering problem it cannot win. Same pattern as keycloak-operator's admin-secret.
#
# ⚠️ CONSEQUENCE: the token lands in Terraform state. That is the existing trade-off for every
# secret this project renders through a chart template, not a new one — but it is a real reason to
# use a NARROWLY SCOPED token. This one needs exactly two permissions:
#     Zone > Zone > Read      (find the zone)
#     Zone > DNS  > Edit      (write and delete _acme-challenge TXT records)
# scoped to the single zone. Do NOT reuse the tunnel/Terraform token: that one also carries
# `Account > Access: Apps and Policies > Edit`, and Cloudflare Access is currently the only control
# keeping the IN-20 header-forgery hole off the public internet. A compromised cert-manager pod
# holding that token could delete the very policy protecting these hostnames.
#
# Rendered only when parameters.common.dns01.enabled is true, so merging this changes nothing
# until an environment opts in.
%{ if try(parameters.common.dns01.enabled, false) ~}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: cloudflare-dns01-token
  namespace: ${namespace}
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: terraform
type: Opaque
stringData:
  api-token: ${parameters.common.dns01.api_token}
---
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: ${try(parameters.common.dns01.issuer_name, "letsencrypt-dns01")}
spec:
  acme:
    # ⚠️ START ON STAGING. Let's Encrypt production has rate limits that are easy to burn while
    # iterating on a solver config, and a lockout blocks this work for a week. Staging issues an
    # UNTRUSTED cert — good enough to prove the whole chain works, and the only thing it cannot
    # prove is that a pod trusts the result. Switch to production only once staging issues cleanly.
    server: ${try(parameters.common.dns01.acme_server, "https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory")}
    email: ${parameters.common.dns01.email}
    privateKeySecretRef:
      name: ${try(parameters.common.dns01.issuer_name, "letsencrypt-dns01")}-account-key
    solvers:
      - dns01:
          cloudflare:
            apiTokenSecretRef:
              name: cloudflare-dns01-token
              key: api-token
%{ if try(length(parameters.common.dns01.zones), 0) > 0 ~}
        selector:
          dnsZones:
%{ for z in parameters.common.dns01.zones ~}
            - ${z}
%{ endfor ~}
%{ endif ~}
%{ endif ~}
